SPHERICAL HALF-POLYGONS — cut a Platonic face in two, enumerate what the halves tile
====================================================================================
AL's idea (2026-08-14): halve a regular tile and the halves carry edges of two or three lengths, so
they need edge types — and they can be reassembled in ways the whole tile could not.

THE MODEL, and why it is not the existing spherical one. develop_spherical.py enumerates polyhedra
with FLAT regular faces under positive-defect vertices, solving for the one edge arc rho that closes
every vertex. None of that applies to a fixed-angle spherical tile:
  * it is RIGID — a spherical triangle's sides follow from its angles — so there is nothing to solve
  * it is SCALENE, so the arc is per-dart, read from the alphabet's own ETYPE
  * the vertex rule is EUCLIDEAN (angles sum to exactly a full turn) and that is correct: on a
    fixed-angle tiling the curvature sits in the FACES, not at the vertices. The surface is a sphere
    by Gauss-Bonnet on the faces, total excess = 4pi.
That last point BOUNDS EACH PALETTE BEFORE THE SEARCH RUNS: excess = tile area, so the tile count is
4pi / excess exactly, and any development closing with another count is wrong, not merely different.

CHANGES
  gen_alphabet.py  the composite angle-sum assert is curvature-aware, gated on a new palette field
                   `tileGeometry` (euclidean | spherical | hyperbolic) so a typo still fails loudly.
                   GUARD: make check-regular PASS byte-identical; tri45 / planigon / penrose / fdsq2
                   all regenerate with unchanged configs and A6 PASS.
  develop_sph_half.py  NEW. develop_spherical's SO(3) flood with rho and alpha made per-dart. Side
                   arcs are DERIVED from the angles by the polar law of cosines and cross-checked
                   against the palette's declared degrees — the cube's arcs are arccos(+-1/3) and the
                   icosahedron's are worse, so hand-typing them was a bug waiting to happen.
  run-sph-half.sh  NEW driver: alphabet -> solve -> prune -> develop on S(2).

RESULTS — three palettes, all faces certified exactly congruent to the tile
  palette         tile                  D   tiles   found                     arc/angle err
  sph-oct-half    45-90-90  (2,2,4)     8    16     2   k=2, 3                4.0e-10
  sph-cube-half   60-120-60             6    12     7   k=2 x5, 4, 6          3.9e-10
  sph-ico-half    36-90-72  CHIRAL     20    40     2   k=3, 6                4.4e-10
  Every one: Euler characteristic 2, tile count exactly the area bound, and 3,000 random points per
  tiling each covered by EXACTLY ONE face (the first coverage test was wrong — the sign condition for
  a spherical triangle is invariant under p -> -p, so it counted each face and its antipode).

INDEPENDENT VERIFICATION — the whole point, and it is exact
  These are not self-checks. Each palette's answer is predicted by a separate group computation and
  the engine reproduces it including the per-k split and every symmetry-group order.
  * cube. The diagonal's endpoints are cube VERTICES, so no vertex can be a T-junction and ALL 2^6
    diagonal choices tile. Orbits under the cube's 48 symmetries: 7. Stabiliser orders 24,12,12,8,4,4,2
    giving k = 2,2,2,2,2,4,6. ENGINE: 7 tilings, k = 2x5, 4, 6, measured |G| = 24,12,12,8,4,4,2. EXACT.
    (An earlier run to kmax=5 found only 6 and looked like a mismatch; the 7th is the k=6 one.)
  * octahedron. The altitude's foot is an edge MIDPOINT, so edge-to-edge forces the neighbour across
    that edge to cut there too: a tiling IS a perfect matching of the face graph. The octahedron's
    face graph has 9 perfect matchings, collapsing to 2 tilings, |G| = 32 (k=2, 3 matchings) and
    |G| = 8 (k=3, 6 matchings). ENGINE: exactly those two, k and |G| both. EXACT.
  * icosahedron. Same matching argument on the dodecahedral graph: 36 perfect matchings -> 2 tilings,
    |G| = 20 (k=3) and |G| = 4 (k=6). ENGINE: exactly those two. EXACT.
  ⚑ One wrong turn recorded: I first computed k as vertex orbits under the stabiliser of the matching
  INSIDE the polyhedron's symmetry group and got k=3 for both octahedron tilings, against the engine's
  2 and 3. The engine was right. A tiling's symmetry group need not sit inside the polyhedron's — the
  |G|=32 one does not even divide 48, because a symmetry may carry a pair of halves that forms an
  octahedron face onto a pair that does not.

WHAT IT SHOWS
  Halving is not a relabelling: the cube's single face-diagonal cut yields SEVEN distinct tilings where
  the cube itself is one, and the halves genuinely orient differently, exactly as AL predicted. The
  icosahedron palette is also the first CHIRAL spherical tile here — three distinct angles, three
  distinct sides, mirror twin a separate alphabet symbol — which only runs because of the sided-classes
  repair of 2026-08-13.

NOT DONE
  No shelf. These are 11 tilings in S(2) cells (vertices as unit 3-vectors + index rings); the atlas's
  spherical shelf has its own record shape and this has not been wired into it.

ON THE SHELF (2026-08-14, later)
  All 11 ship under Spherical -> Tilings -> Board, as three boards beside the 3.4.n.4 family:
  "Octahedron halved (16 tiles)" 2, "Cube halved (12 tiles)" 7, "Icosahedron halved (40 tiles)" 2.

  NO NEW RENDERER. A record is a `SphPolyPattern`, the type the 3.4.n.4 spherical shelf already uses —
  explicit per-record geometry, unit vectors and face rings — so populating that field routes the cards,
  the thumbnails and /play through the three.js sphere every other spherical shelf draws on. What is new
  is only board metadata (lib/tilings/sph-half.ts) and an emitter (emit_sph_half_shelf.py), which
  re-certifies everything before writing: Euler 2, the area-bound tile count, every face congruent to
  the tile, every sampled point covered exactly once.

  FILL BY FACE ORBIT, not by polygon size. The default key is the polygon size, which is right for the
  3.4.n.4 boards and useless here — every face is a triangle, so the whole solid collapsed into ONE
  neutral grey (tileColor deliberately mutes a single-group solid). Records now carry `fillGroup`, the
  face's orbit under the tiling's own isometries; it stays one colour exactly when the tiling really is
  tile-transitive (the |G|=24 cube) and separates 10 classes on the k=6 icosahedron.
    tiling                 face orbits / tiles   |G|
    shoct-half-2-00001       1 / 16               32
    shoct-half-3-00001       2 / 16                8
    shcube-half-2-00002      1 / 12               24
    shcube-half-6-00001      7 / 12                2
    shico-half-3-00001       2 / 40               20
    shico-half-6-00001      10 / 40                4

  ⚑ BUG AL FOUND AND I SHIPPED: clicking one of these on /library landed on t1001. `_play-client`
  decides whether to fetch the spherical shelves with `requestedKey.startsWith("sp")`, and these ids
  begin "sh<board>-", so the shelf was never fetched, the key never resolved, and the selection fell
  through to sorted[0] silently. Now matched against SPH_HALF_BOARDS rather than a bare "sh" prefix.
  This is the THIRD shelf to need a line there (mixed and scaled hit it on 2026-08-13) — the fallback
  is silent by design, so a new shelf never fails loudly, it just lands on the wrong tiling.

  ⚑ Caught by the guard, not by me: my first version of the shared `sphPolyScene` computed the group
  count as max(key)+1, which drops a polygon size a board DECLARES but a given record does not use.
  lib/tilings/sph-poly.test.ts failed on it. The size path now allocates stats.sizes.length groups
  exactly as before, and only the fillGroup path uses max+1.

