FOURTH BOARD: THE DODECAHEDRON (2026-08-14, later)
  A pentagon has no halving DIAGONAL — a diagonal cuts off a triangle and leaves a quadrilateral — so
  the cut is the MIRROR, from a vertex to the midpoint of the opposite edge, and the halves are
  60-120-120-90 QUADRILATERALS. Chiral. Sides: the dodecahedron edge 41.810315 twice, half of it
  20.905157, and the mirror segment 69.094843 = 90 minus the half-edge exactly. The long edge is again
  exactly twice the short one. Excess 30 deg = pi/6, so exactly 4pi/(pi/6) = 24 tiles.

  THE FIRST NON-TRIANGLE, and it forced a real change. A spherical n-gon has 2n-3 degrees of freedom
  against n angles: at n=3 the angles pin it (rigid, sides derivable) and at n=4 one parameter is free,
  so a 60-120-120-90 spherical quadrilateral is a FAMILY, not a shape. This board therefore DECLARES its
  arcs, and the check that they are right is the development, which will not close on a tile that does
  not exist. Two more places assumed triangles and were generalised: the symmetry solver (three image
  points determine the map, so take 3 from a face, not len(face) — a 3x4 matrix is not invertible) and
  the coverage test (any convex n-gon, not a 3-vertex sign test).

  RESULT, and it matches the independent prediction EXACTLY:
    the cut's foot is an edge midpoint, so a tiling is again a perfect matching of the face graph — here
    the ICOSAHEDRAL graph (12 vertices, 5-regular). 125 perfect matchings -> 5 distinct tilings, with
    |G| = 24, 12, 6, 4, 2 and k = 3, 4, 5, 7, 13.
    ENGINE: 5 tilings, one at each of k = 3, 4, 5, 7, 13 and NOTHING at any other k up to 13, measured
    |G| = 24, 12, 6, 4, 2. Every one Euler 2, F=24 at the area bound, arc/angle error ~3e-10.
  ⚑ A first run to k=6 found only 3 and looked like a mismatch against the predicted 5. The missing two
  are at k=7 and k=13. Third time this session that "the count is short" meant "search deeper" — on the
  cube it was k=6, on {3,7} it was k=6, here k=13.

  Board totals now: oct 2, cube 7, ico 2, dodec 5 = 16 spherical half-tilings, every count reproduced
  exactly by an independent group computation, k split and symmetry orders included.
